OxC3 web frontend, outstanding work
===================================

What is left to do on shader.oxsomi.com and the compiler paths behind it. STATUS.md holds the
capability table and the wider roadmap; this file is the working list for the frontend, kept at the
level of "what would someone pick up next".

An item leaves this list by landing WITH its tests and its documentation, per FOR_CONTRIBUTORS.md.


Before this goes out
--------------------

- web/* still needs a manual read-through by Niels. These are what is left:

    isa.js, asmmap.js, symbols.js, editor.js, compile.js, gen_mock_data.js, pipeline.js, diff.js,
    frontend_unit.js, wasm.js, mock.js, mock_formats.js, smoke.js, wasm_smoke.js, intellisense.js,
    app.js, api.js, inspect.js

  api.js and app.js carry the most: api.js is the contract everything downstream believes, and app.js
  is a third of the frontend and holds most of the places that build HTML. The share restore inside
  app.js is the only path a stranger's bytes reach, so it is worth reading against the validation in
  util.js rather than on its own.
- It needs an Astra review as well.


Next big thing
--------------

- Live AMD ISA in the browser, through a spirv2isa Mesa fork compiled to wasm. This is the last
  greyed-out capability on the page: the ISA tab works on desktop through amdllpc + amdgpu-dis, and
  in the browser it refuses because those cannot be spawned. Marked in STATUS.md as needing the fork.


The VS Code host
----------------

What it is for decides most of the scope. Hover and go-to-definition on real files is HLSL-LSP's
ground and it does that well. What nothing else offers is the oiSH, pipeline and ISA views on the
user's own project against a real device, so the host is built for those, and the editor
intelligence it gets is whatever the existing symbol path gives for free. web/README.md describes
the design; this is the order to build it in.

Already in place:

- The page side of the transport. wasm_rpc.js forwards every OxAPI call uniformly, so a postMessage
  transport to the extension is that file with the Worker swapped out.
- Extension-local resources: web/vendor is exactly what the webview's CSP demands.
- Document reads: `file data --json` gives every oiSH, oiSR, oiSP, oiSB and oiPL as the page's own
  document, so the host writes a file and asks.
- Detection: OxAPI.host and capabilities(). The vscode branch currently promises liveIsa and
  offlineIsa unconditionally, which step 1 turns into an answer from the host.

Steps, each done with its test:

1. Transport and a walking skeleton. Extension scaffold (package.json, activation, one command that
   opens the webview), the CSP with local resources, vscode_rpc.js mirroring wasm_rpc.js, and
   capabilities() answered by the host after probing the binary (`isa devices`, whether RGA is
   bundled). Done when the page opens in the webview on the mock tier and reports what the host can
   do. Test: jsdom with acquireVsCodeApi stubbed, asserting every OxAPI method reaches the transport
   and none falls back to a mock. That test is what makes a new page feature fail loudly in the host
   instead of quietly mocking.
2. Compile and inspect over the verbs that exist. compileFile and compileRaw over `shader compile`,
   reflectSymbols over `shader reflect-symbols` then `file data --json`, the parse and header reads
   over `file data --json`, extractBinary over `file data --bin`, disassemble, assemble, validate,
   entrypoints and combine over their shader verbs, snapshots over `file to` and `file from`, the
   ISA calls over `isa`. One temp directory per session, one spawn per call, stdout captured, the
   binary's errors mapped onto the page's diagnostics shape. Done when the samples compile and every
   tab renders from the native binary.
3. The verbs the page needs and the CLI lacks. Three are already listed under Compiler and CLI:
   reflecting a bare .spv or .dxil, assembling into a real oiSH, and the parse-only permutation
   listing. New here: `--json` on the vocabularies (the annotation enums, the pipeline field table,
   the builtin include table), since every dropdown on the page is built from them, and pipeline
   derive, supply and print as verbs of their own rather than inside `isa`. Each ships with a
   tools/test.py case.
4. Real files. The page owns a virtual project; the extension maps it onto the workspace: the active
   file's folder as the root, includes resolved against it, a recompile on save. Decide up front that
   the page is a viewer over workspace files rather than a second editor, so edits flow one way and
   there is no merge to get wrong. This is where the two hosts diverge and where most of the bugs
   will live.
5. Process lifetime. One spawn per call is right for a compile and wrong for reflection on every
   keystroke. A persistent process with a request loop (a `--server` mode on the CLI) replaces the
   per-call spawn for reflect-symbols and parse. Gated on step 4 showing the latency actually hurts,
   since HLSL-LSP already covers the keystroke path and this host is not competing there.
6. Live ISA on the device: `isa disassemble -asic live` with `-pso-output` through the host. This is
   the feature no other tool has and the reason the extension exists. Done when changing a pipeline
   field in the page reruns the live route and the ISA diff shows the change.
7. Semantic highlighting in the editor itself, off by default behind one setting. oiSR already carries
   what a token provider needs (a kind and a source span per node), so the mapping is small, and the
   page's own editor already colours from it. The reason to offer it is not a second general
   highlighter: it is that these tokens know what the compiled binary did, so a register no binary
   used, a symbol outside the selected permutation, and anything from a builtin include can each read
   differently. That is the colouring nobody else can produce.
   Default off because a token provider is global to the language rather than scoped to the webview,
   so installing this must never change how someone's existing setup renders, and two providers for
   one language is not a fight a user can reason about (HLSL-LSP already documents that collision with
   HLSL Tools). No TextMate grammar is shipped either: the static layer is where conflicts are worst
   and where there is nothing to add.
   HLSL-LSP running alongside is the expected setup, not a fallback: it owns the language surface and
   this owns the binaries, the pipelines and the ISA. Taking .hlsl over is for the project that writes
   the oxc:: annotations, where the entrypoint selector is the thing being improved: permutations,
   their stages and which binary each one produces come out of this tree and cannot come out of a
   frontend that does not read the annotations.
   Two rules make it usable rather than annoying. Tokens come from the last reflection that produced a
   tree, so a file mid-edit keeps its previous colouring instead of losing it, and the provider says so
   rather than silently lagging. And it stays gated on step 5, since colouring on every keystroke is
   the case that needs the persistent process.

Sizing: 1 and 2 are days, 3 is CLI work with tests and docs, 4 is the bulk, 6 is small once 4
exists, 7 is small in itself but only sensible after 5. Rename, references and signature help stay
out; they are under Editor intelligence and gated on the DXC IntelliSense seam either way.


Editor intelligence
-------------------

- Let the IntelliSense binary dropdown drive the selected binary, so the SPIR-V and DXIL views follow
  whichever permutation the editor is on. The catch is a recompile whose entrypoints no longer match
  the previous one's; that case needs a stale marker near the compile button rather than a silent
  mismatch.

- HLSL builtin intrinsics in hover and completion, with real signatures and a short description.
  Today hover resolves symbols the reflector emits, so a user's own declarations work and every
  builtin (mul, saturate, InterlockedAdd) is silent, which reads as a gap rather than a boundary.
- Doc comments: carry the comment above a declaration through the reflector into the hover card.
- Longer term, gated on a DXC IntelliSense seam: signature help while typing, an outline view, and
  rename / find-references.


Compiler and CLI, as the page uses them
---------------------------------------

- Extension x model validation table (for example PAQ below its minimum shader model), shared by the
  CLI and the web assemble card, so an illegal combination greys out instead of failing late.
- `shader assemble` into a real oiSH (an SHFile built from a binary plus an identifier). The web
  round-trip check would then refuse rather than warn.
- Reflect a bare .spv / .dxil has no CLI verb yet, though the page does it. The web CLI map says so.
- Parse-only permutation listing, so the page's "IntelliSense follows" picker can fill before any
  compile. Compiler_parse already yields the SHEntryRuntime this needs.
- Descriptor layout overrides in the pipeline editor: the page shows the derived oiPL read only, and
  editing a row and supplying one is missing.
- The page derives layouts with no excluded registers, so the runtime's own registers (the bindless
  arrays, the per frame globals) show up as rows the device would own. The CLI's exclusion list should
  reach the module's derive call.
- A visitor pasting an older shader that indexes samplers dynamically sees "undeclared identifier
  samplerUniform", because the bindless sampler array is opt in through
  [[oxc::extension("DynamicSamplers")]]; the diagnostic could say why.
- build_web.py --skip_host_package fails with conan's own "Unable to find oxc3/<version>" whenever the
  version moved and the cached host package is older; a hint naming --host_package_only would save a
  puzzled minute.
- docs/roadmap.md and the roadmap section of STATUS.md overlap; one should absorb the other, and this
  file should point at whichever survives.
- SBFile_combine mismatch errors should name the buffer and both sizes.
- coop_vec compiles to different DXIL from run to run. The same source, compiled repeatedly through the
  CLI, yields several distinct oiSH: the container hash moves and so do scattered bytes inside the DXIL
  bitcode, while SPIR-V stays stable and every other sample is stable on both backends. The sample
  suite therefore pins coop_vec's SPIR-V and skips its DXIL. Worth a reduced repro against the DXC fork
  the way the multi-base and new/delete ones were done, then re-enable the pin.
- No way to request a SPIR-V version. DXIL has the shader model (`[[oxc::model]]` and the CLI's
  `-model`), and SPIR-V has no equivalent, so a shader cannot say which SPIR-V version it targets and
  the toolchain's default is the only one available. The page's validator already reads the version
  out of a module's header, so the reader half exists; what is missing is the annotation, the CLI
  flag, the oiSH field to carry it, and passing it through to the SPIR-V backend.


JSON serialization belongs in the formats
-----------------------------------------

Each format writes its own JSON view through OxC3_formats_json (formats/json/json_writer.h) and
src/tools/oxc3_wasm only frames it. What is left is reaching those views from the CLI and widening them.

- oiSP's specializations come out of the JSON view as bare numbers, the enum value rather than the name the
  CLI print uses, which makes a stored pipeline hard to read and edit as a document.
- Under `--verbose`, DXIL and SPIR-V could carry a disassembly. In the long run that could replace the
  binary goldens, or sit alongside them. It follows oiDL's rule for contents: the shape by default, the
  bytes only when asked, and a stated omission with the size when they are too big to carry.
- It might make sense to replace the normal print system with this. oiCA and oiDL have their views
  already; oiBC and the image formats are what is left, and each one's shape is straightforward.
- A deserializer is needed at some point too, not just a writer.
- Base64 as a second encoding for bytes in a document. Hex is what the writer has, because it matches
  what `file data` prints for binary and stays readable at the sizes contents actually travel at (a
  held entry, so at most the format's medium tier). It costs 2x where base64 costs 1.33x, which only
  starts mattering if whole binaries travel, which is the --verbose disassembly item. There is no
  base64 codec in the tree, so this is an encoder and its tests, and a decoder too once anything reads
  a document back.


Reflection and the JSON views
-----------------------------

- Nodes should know their children when the AST is built. Emitting a node's child list currently scans
  every node looking for one whose parent is this node, which is quadratic in the node count and is by
  far the heaviest part of writing an oiSR document.
- The page always folds builtin includes in the symbol tree. Expanding them should be a switch; the
  document already carries every node with its builtin flag, so this is page work only.
- The JSON strings should reserve up front, so a document does not reallocate repeatedly while the
  first fields are written.
- The mock tier still invents four things: the regexes that guess which extensions a source uses, the
  SPIR-V capability names it prints per extension, the DXC argument line the Command tab reconstructs,
  and the wave-op model bumps. Each is a stand-in for something only the module can answer; the
  Command tab's is listed as not wired.
- Texture and StructuredBuffer inner type names: IHLSLReflection appears to give us the inner type,
  and hover does not show it.


Maintenance
-----------

- Split src/tools/oxc3_wasm/wasm_main.c into one file per export family; it holds every export in one
  file.
- Cap the size of an oiCA the page will accept, so a shared archive cannot flood the receiver's memory
  just because someone packed a large one. The number needs choosing; the check itself is a line.

- Split web/js/app.js into feature modules. Share and restore, the binary strip, and the tabs are the
  first three; new features already land as their own modules.
- Minifying the page's own js and css would take another fifth off what a visitor downloads (155 KB
  brotli today, ~124 KB with comments and indentation gone). It needs a minifier as a dev dependency
  and a place to put the output, since index.html names the sources; the compression siblings already
  took the larger win.
- UBSan (clang, -fsanitize=function) reports calls through mismatched function pointer types in the
  graphics C/C++ interop, because the oxc:: C++ twins of the C structs make the callback signatures
  distinct types. Harmless on current ABIs, but it is 25 reports of noise in every sanitizer run.
- Split the build scripts, after the release rather than before it: this touches every build path and
  CI with nothing to catch a mistake, so it is the wrong thing to be holding while shipping.
  build_common.py (1113 lines, 40 functions) goes first, since the other three depend on it, and it
  already reads as three concerns that share only the run helper: host and toolchain discovery
  (platform names, compiler resolution and version detection, profiles), conan mechanics (recipe
  constants, hashing, the generator prune, create-if-changed), and the host tool package with its
  dependency builds. A package behind the same name moves the code without touching a call site.
  build_web.py (852) and build_android.py (970) come after. In build_web the hosting half is the clean
  seam: the vendor fetch, compression, the deploy package and the local server have no relationship to
  the conan build above them, and they are about a third of the file.


Parked
------

- Mouse back/forward navigating page state.
- GLSL input: HLSL only by design, stated in the docs.

- Slang input. Not a second front end inside the compiler: the annotations, the register model, the
  oiSB layouts and the oiSR symbol tree are all shaped by DXC's reflection, so a Slang path means a
  second implementation producing the same SHEntryRuntime and oiSH, and every divergence between the
  two surfaces as a user's bug rather than a build error. It would also mean two HLSL front ends in
  one wasm module, against a download the page's whole pitch rests on. ECompilerFormat has one member
  and nothing dispatches on it, so the enum is not the cost. What Slang users can be given instead
  costs nothing here: they run slangc themselves and bring the SPIR-V or DXIL, which the page already
  validates, reflects and disassembles, and `shader assemble` into a real oiSH closes the rest.
  Revisit when engines that matter here ship Slang as their default authoring language.
- Metal and WebGPU output is a separate question from the above, and Slang is a detour for it: the
  SPIR-V already exists, so SPIRV-Cross and Tint translate what is there instead of replacing the
  front end (DXIL -> AIR also exists, but can't be in the browser).
- Clang HLSL as a second module, which also subsumes the parked side-by-side against upstream DXC.
  Same language and same annotations, so unlike Slang it changes nothing about what a user writes,
  and tracking it is eventually maintenance rather than a feature. What decides it is reflection
  parity: DXC is used here for IDxcContainerReflection and IDxcIntelliSense as much as for codegen,
  and a path that lacks an equivalent surface does not replace DXC, it adds a weaker one beside it.
  Shipping it as its own module rather than linking both into one keeps the download honest, since
  wasmload.js already picks a module folder per version; comparing the fork against upstream on the
  same shader then becomes a dropdown. Compression is not the argument either way: the shared
  ancestry is in the sources, not in the compiled bytes, and memory and instantiation cost both
  modules whatever the transfer compressed to.


Notes for whoever picks this up
-------------------------------

- This machine has no node or npm on PATH. The emsdk's node is the only one, and everything under
  web/dev needs it.
- The wasm bundle and the desktop build are two configurations of one toolchain. Alternating build.py
  and build_web.py needs no wipe, because the host tools get their own output tree through
  OxC3OutputTag. If a link ever shows "multiple definition" walls in graphics code nobody touched, a
  tree is being shared again and the tag is the fix.
- The DXC fork's dev tree and the three files that pin it are described in CLAUDE.md and the fork's
  own notes. Fork edits carry an `HLSL Change:` marker at most, matching upstream LLVM's sparseness.
